\section{A Benchmark Is a Claim About a Workload}

In \textit{Performance of Lisp Systems}, Richard P. Gabriel and
Larry M. Masinter distinguish what can be said about underlying
hardware, individual operations, and larger Lisp facilities. A
timing of a primitive does not predict the duration of a whole
request, but a whole-program timing without analysis rarely
explains which design to change. Gabriel's \textit{Performance
and Evaluation of Lisp Systems} illustrates this by pairing
benchmark code with counts of significant operations, discussion
of implementations, and limitations of the measurements. Gabriel's
later essay \textit{Lisp: Good News, Bad News, How to Win Big}
also illustrates the need to separate technical comparisons on
selected kernels from claims about delivery and adoption.

There are empirical questions for which a timer is the wrong
instrument. Douglas W. Clark and C. Cordell Green's
\textit{An Empirical Study of List Structure in Lisp} examined
retained list structures from five Interlisp-10 programs. Cdr
links often reached physically nearby cells, and references to
atoms were concentrated on a subset of atoms. This supported
considering compact representations. The observations are about
the structures found at the measurement points, not about all
allocations made during execution. Repeating the study on a
workload dominated by recently allocated transient objects would
require an allocation trace or samples taken over the run.

\section{Worked Experiment: Function Calls Versus Consing}

Gabriel's \texttt{tak} benchmark gives a narrow question:
how costly is a deeply branching computation dominated by
recursive calls and small-integer arithmetic?

\begin{verbatim}
(defun tak (x y z)
  (if (not (< y x))
      z
      (tak (tak (1- x) y z)
           (tak (1- y) z x)
           (tak (1- z) x y))))
\end{verbatim}

With arguments \(18,12,6\), the expected result is \(7\).
Gabriel counts \(63{,}609\) calls in the direct evaluation,
including the initial call. Confirm the result on the exact
code to be measured; check the count in a separate instrumented
run so that counting does not become part of the timing.
Provide the inputs through the same driver for each system,
and consume the result so a compiler cannot simply eliminate
the computation. Record whether arithmetic is performed by
generic operations or specialized on a justified declaration.
A change in compilation policy is a change in the experiment.

Pair this with a second workload that builds a list of \(n\)
pairs from changing input, deliberately retains a bounded
fraction of batches across collections, and verifies a length
and checksum after the run. Vsevolod Domkin's
\textit{Programming Algorithms in Lisp} discusses performance
in the context of concrete Common Lisp data structures. Here,
record bytes allocated and heap size after collection as well
as elapsed time. Different timings at equal allocation can
come from different survival rates or heap policies, whereas
different allocation with the same output may show an
optimization, for example removing temporary structures.

Domkin demonstrates the difference with allocation profiling and a
before-and-after collection comparison. Neither total allocation nor
heap size after a collection is peak memory use. For instance, a
driver that allocates one million pairs while retaining only the
last hundred can reuse storage throughout the run; one that retains
every pair cannot, even though they both execute a million
\texttt{cons} calls. Check that the driver really retains the
stated number of pairs before attributing a timing difference to the
collector.

\section{A Run Protocol That Readers Can Reproduce}

Report the implementation and version, target and operating
system, optimization and safety settings, hardware, and inputs.
Distinguish startup and compilation from steady execution:
both can matter, but they answer different user questions.
State heap sizes, warm-up and collection policies, and the
number of independent runs. Report wall time separately from
CPU time and collection pauses; use repeated runs and report
spread, not just the best observation. If a system cannot
report allocation or pause time comparably, disclose the
missing data rather than reading it as zero.

Neither a \texttt{tak} win nor a consing win explains an
application's slow path without further evidence. Chapter~31
of \textit{The Modern Compiler Dossier} proposes controlled
profiling interventions for this reason. Use the measured
collector design of Chapter~\ref{ch:ext-157} to formulate a
hypothesis, then vary one factor and test for it. The final
claim should name the workload, runtime configuration, and
quantity that actually changed.
